            buckets: ["Absolute is wrong", "Absolute is right"],
            items: [
              { text: "A habit can never be proved without an eyewitness.", bucket: "Absolute is wrong", why: "FRE 406: habit is admissible whether or not corroborated or witnessed." },
              { text: "A defendant sued in its own State's court may never remove the case.", bucket: "Absolute is wrong", why: "A federal-question case is removable by a forum defendant; the rule limits only diversity-only removal." },
              { text: "A modification always needs new consideration.", bucket: "Absolute is wrong", why: "UCC 2-209(1) dispenses with consideration for goods." },
              { text: "Under the MBE's default Torts rule, a plaintiff who is at fault can never recover.", bucket: "Absolute is wrong", why: "The default is pure comparative fault: her fault reduces the award. Only a stem that names contributory negligence makes 'never' true." },
              { text: "Police must always give warnings before asking a suspect any question.", bucket: "Absolute is wrong", why: "Only custodial interrogation triggers Miranda." },
              { text: "The parties can never confer subject-matter jurisdiction on a federal court by consent.", bucket: "Absolute is right", why: "Subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be created by consent or waiver; it can be raised at any time." },
              { text: "Under the Restatement (Second), a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds must be signed by or on behalf of the party to be charged.", bucket: "Absolute is right", why: "Common-law memorandum rule (Rest. (2d) Contracts §131): the party to be charged, or that party's agent, must sign. UCC 2-201(2) can bind a merchant who does not object to a confirmation, so scope the absolute to the law the stem names." },
              { text: "Miranda warnings are required only before custodial interrogation.", bucket: "Absolute is right", why: "Custody plus interrogation is the whole trigger; 'only' is accurate." },
              { text: "The prosecution must prove every element of the crime beyond a reasonable doubt.", bucket: "Absolute is right", why: "In re Winship (1970): due process requires proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every fact necessary to constitute the crime charged." },
            ],
            say: "Absolute words are a flag, not a verdict: test them against the rule. Some rules really are absolute.",
            hook: "'Every' and 'must' are right when the rule has no exception: scope the absolute to the law the stem names.",
